1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
The canister under this camping stove holds butane,

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:04,640
often mixed with propane.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:06,840
That name isn't a brand.

4
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,400
It's a code: but means four carbons, and

5
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:13,120
ane means only single bonds between them.

6
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:16,720
Chemists name carbon compounds this way, so a

7
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:20,160
name like three-methylpent-one-ene is really a set of

8
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:21,440
drawing instructions.

9
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:23,760
So how do you read a name like

10
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:25,840
that, and write one from a drawing?

11
00:00:27,067 --> 00:00:29,226
You know from Year 11 that carbon has

12
00:00:29,307 --> 00:00:33,146
four valence electrons, so each carbon forms four

13
00:00:33,227 --> 00:00:34,666
covalent bonds.

14
00:00:34,987 --> 00:00:37,226
Carbon can bond to other carbons, so it

15
00:00:37,307 --> 00:00:40,186
builds long chains, and a chain can grow

16
00:00:40,267 --> 00:00:41,226
branches.

17
00:00:41,547 --> 00:00:44,266
That's why there are millions of carbon compounds,

18
00:00:44,347 --> 00:00:46,106
and why each one needs a name that

19
00:00:46,187 --> 00:00:48,026
points to just one structure.

20
00:00:48,667 --> 00:00:50,586
Real chains aren't straight, either.

21
00:00:50,987 --> 00:00:53,946
The bonds spread out, so the carbons zigzag.

22
00:00:55,167 --> 00:00:57,006
There are three common ways to draw a

23
00:00:57,087 --> 00:00:57,846
chain.

24
00:00:58,127 --> 00:01:01,166
A full structural formula shows every atom and

25
00:01:01,247 --> 00:01:02,206
every bond.

26
00:01:02,447 --> 00:01:05,086
A condensed formula writes each carbon with its

27
00:01:05,247 --> 00:01:08,526
hydrogens: C H three, C H two, C

28
00:01:08,647 --> 00:01:10,686
H two, C H three.

29
00:01:10,727 --> 00:01:13,806
A skeletal formula keeps just the zigzag.

30
00:01:13,887 --> 00:01:16,446
Every corner and every end is a carbon,

31
00:01:16,487 --> 00:01:18,526
and the hydrogens are left out.

32
00:01:18,607 --> 00:01:20,446
You put them back in your head, enough

33
00:01:20,487 --> 00:01:23,166
to give each carbon four bonds: three on

34
00:01:23,207 --> 00:01:25,566
an end carbon, two on a corner, and

35
00:01:25,647 --> 00:01:27,326
one where a branch joins.

36
00:01:28,534 --> 00:01:30,933
Now the naming rules, one at a time.

37
00:01:31,494 --> 00:01:33,853
Rule one: the start of the name, the

38
00:01:33,894 --> 00:01:37,173
stem, counts the carbons in the main chain.

39
00:01:37,254 --> 00:01:41,253
Meth is one, eth two, prop three, but

40
00:01:41,454 --> 00:01:41,973
four.

41
00:01:42,294 --> 00:01:45,093
A way to remember those: Monkeys Eat Peeled

42
00:01:45,174 --> 00:01:46,173
Bananas.

43
00:01:46,534 --> 00:01:49,573
From five, the stems match shapes you know:

44
00:01:49,774 --> 00:01:53,013
pent as in pentagon, hex as in hexagon,

45
00:01:53,054 --> 00:01:55,653
hept as in heptagon, and oct, as in

46
00:01:55,734 --> 00:01:57,173
octagon, for eight.

47
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:01,200
Rule two: the ending tells you the bonds

48
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:02,480
between carbons.

49
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:05,520
Ane means only single bonds.

50
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:09,200
Chemists call that saturated: each carbon holds as

51
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:10,880
many hydrogens as it can.

52
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,920
Ene means there's a carbon to carbon double

53
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,640
bond, and yne means a triple bond.

54
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:18,640
Those are unsaturated.

55
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,920
To remember the order, a, e and y

56
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,880
go alphabetically, just as one, two and three

57
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:26,160
bonds go in order.

58
00:02:27,367 --> 00:02:28,086
Rule three:

59
00:02:28,727 --> 00:02:31,526
find the longest continuous chain of carbons.

60
00:02:32,007 --> 00:02:34,486
That's the main chain, also called the parent

61
00:02:34,567 --> 00:02:37,046
chain, and it can bend on the page.

62
00:02:37,367 --> 00:02:38,166
Look at this one.

63
00:02:38,567 --> 00:02:40,726
It's drawn as four carbons in a row,

64
00:02:40,847 --> 00:02:43,126
with two more hanging down, so you might

65
00:02:43,207 --> 00:02:44,966
call it two-ethylbutane.

66
00:02:45,527 --> 00:02:47,606
But trace a path that turns down into

67
00:02:47,687 --> 00:02:50,006
that branch, and you get five carbons.

68
00:02:50,487 --> 00:02:52,966
So the main chain is pentane, and the

69
00:02:53,047 --> 00:02:55,126
real branch is just one carbon.

70
00:02:56,334 --> 00:02:58,733
A branch is named from its carbons, with

71
00:02:58,894 --> 00:03:02,013
yl on the end: one carbon is methyl,

72
00:03:02,094 --> 00:03:03,213
two is ethyl.

73
00:03:03,934 --> 00:03:06,573
Rule four: number the main chain from the

74
00:03:06,654 --> 00:03:09,133
end nearer the branch, so the branch gets

75
00:03:09,214 --> 00:03:11,853
the lowest number, and everyone writes the same

76
00:03:11,934 --> 00:03:12,333
name.

77
00:03:12,814 --> 00:03:14,813
That number is called a locant, and it

78
00:03:14,894 --> 00:03:16,013
says which carbon.

79
00:03:16,574 --> 00:03:19,053
Here the methyl sits on carbon three, from

80
00:03:19,214 --> 00:03:22,613
either end, so this is three-methylpentane.

81
00:03:22,894 --> 00:03:25,293
On this shorter chain, counting from the left

82
00:03:25,414 --> 00:03:28,893
puts the methyl on carbon two: two-methylbutane.

83
00:03:29,374 --> 00:03:31,773
Three-methylbutane counts from the wrong end.

84
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,760
Rule five covers more than one branch.

85
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:38,280
Two of the same get di, as in

86
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:40,760
two, three-dimethylpentane.

87
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:44,280
Commas go between numbers, and a hyphen joins

88
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:45,880
a number to a word.

89
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:49,800
Different branches go in alphabetical order, ignoring di.

90
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:53,480
So this one is three-ethyl-two-methylpentane.

91
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:56,600
E comes before m, even though the methyl

92
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:57,800
has the lower number.

93
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,080
Double and triple bonds add one more step.

94
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:04,360
The main chain has to contain the double

95
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:08,360
bond, because the ending, ene or yne, describes

96
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:09,720
the main chain's bonds.

97
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:12,120
Then you number from the end that gives

98
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:13,320
it the lowest number.

99
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:16,120
That locant goes just before the ending.

100
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:20,040
In but-one-ene, the double bond starts at carbon

101
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:20,520
one.

102
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:23,640
In a skeletal drawing, a double bond counts

103
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:26,360
as two of a carbon's four bonds, so

104
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:29,080
each carbon in it has one fewer hydrogen.

105
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:31,480
Move the double bond to the middle, and

106
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:33,000
you get but-two-ene.

107
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:35,960
Ethene and propene don't need a number at

108
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:38,280
all, because there's only one place the double

109
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:39,240
bond can go.

110
00:04:40,467 --> 00:04:42,066
What if there's a branch as well?

111
00:04:42,547 --> 00:04:44,946
The double bond wins, because it's named in

112
00:04:44,987 --> 00:04:47,186
the ending, so it gets first claim on

113
00:04:47,267 --> 00:04:48,346
the low numbers.

114
00:04:48,627 --> 00:04:50,866
In this chain of five, numbering from the

115
00:04:50,947 --> 00:04:53,586
left puts the double bond on carbon one,

116
00:04:53,667 --> 00:04:57,746
and the methyl on carbon four: four-methylpent-one-ene.

117
00:04:58,227 --> 00:05:00,066
From the right, the methyl would get a

118
00:05:00,227 --> 00:05:01,986
two, but the double bond would get a

119
00:05:02,147 --> 00:05:05,786
four, so two-methylpent-four-ene is wrong.

120
00:05:06,067 --> 00:05:08,786
A triple bond follows the same rules: this

121
00:05:08,867 --> 00:05:10,466
is pent-two-yne.

122
00:05:11,667 --> 00:05:13,346
Names work backwards too.

123
00:05:13,827 --> 00:05:16,386
Take two, two-dimethylpentane.

124
00:05:16,867 --> 00:05:20,146
Draw the main chain first: pent, so five

125
00:05:20,227 --> 00:05:20,786
carbons.

126
00:05:21,267 --> 00:05:23,266
Put two methyls on carbon two.

127
00:05:23,907 --> 00:05:26,946
Then add hydrogens, so every carbon has four

128
00:05:27,027 --> 00:05:27,586
bonds.

129
00:05:28,067 --> 00:05:32,226
Check by counting: seven carbons and sixteen hydrogens.

130
00:05:32,547 --> 00:05:34,626
Count only one of the methyls, and you'd

131
00:05:34,747 --> 00:05:38,386
write C six H fourteen: one carbon and

132
00:05:38,547 --> 00:05:40,946
two hydrogens short, so it can't be this

133
00:05:40,987 --> 00:05:41,666
molecule.

134
00:05:42,867 --> 00:05:45,666
So, to name a chain: find the longest

135
00:05:45,747 --> 00:05:48,666
chain that holds any double or triple bond.

136
00:05:48,947 --> 00:05:51,666
Number it so that bond, then the branches,

137
00:05:51,747 --> 00:05:53,186
get the lowest numbers.

138
00:05:53,427 --> 00:05:55,826
Then write the name in the order branches,

139
00:05:56,027 --> 00:05:57,186
stem, ending.

140
00:05:58,400 --> 00:05:59,040
Your turn.

141
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:02,320
Give the IUPAC name and the molecular formula

142
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:05,040
of the hydrocarbon in this skeletal formula.

143
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:06,800
Pause here to answer.

144
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:19,840
The longest chain has six carbons and holds

145
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:22,320
the double bond, so it's a hexene.

146
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:25,600
Number from the left, nearer the double bond:

147
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:27,920
it starts on carbon two, and the methyl's

148
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,280
on carbon five.

149
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:31,920
That's five-methylhex-two-ene.

150
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:35,600
Each double-bond carbon has just one hydrogen, so

151
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:37,840
it's C seven H fourteen.

152
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:41,600
Two-methylhex-four-ene numbers for the branch, but the double

153
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:42,640
bond wins.

154
00:06:43,867 --> 00:06:44,506
Last one.

155
00:06:45,067 --> 00:06:48,346
A student names a hydrocarbon two-ethylpentane.

156
00:06:48,907 --> 00:06:51,506
Explain why that name is incorrect.

157
00:06:51,787 --> 00:06:54,506
Then give the correct IUPAC name, and draw

158
00:06:54,547 --> 00:06:55,706
the skeletal formula.

159
00:06:56,187 --> 00:06:57,466
Pause here to answer.

160
00:07:07,867 --> 00:07:10,666
Draw what the student described, then trace the

161
00:07:10,827 --> 00:07:13,866
longest chain through the ethyl branch: that's six

162
00:07:13,947 --> 00:07:15,586
carbons, not five.

163
00:07:15,867 --> 00:07:18,026
So the main chain is hexane, with a

164
00:07:18,107 --> 00:07:19,226
methyl branch.

165
00:07:19,547 --> 00:07:21,706
Number from the nearer end, and the methyl

166
00:07:21,787 --> 00:07:25,466
sits on carbon three: three-methylhexane.

167
00:07:25,587 --> 00:07:27,946
Four-methylhexane counts from the wrong end.

168
00:07:29,167 --> 00:07:31,646
So a name tells you the chain length,

169
00:07:31,687 --> 00:07:34,286
the bonds and every branch, and you can

170
00:07:34,367 --> 00:07:36,086
rebuild the molecule from it.

171
00:07:36,367 --> 00:07:37,806
And here's something to think about.

172
00:07:38,367 --> 00:07:41,486
Butane has the formula C four H ten.

173
00:07:42,127 --> 00:07:44,526
Can those same atoms be joined another way?

174
00:07:45,487 --> 00:07:48,526
Next: how the same formula can make different

175
00:07:48,607 --> 00:07:49,486
molecules.

176
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:52,140
Here are the key notes.

177
00:07:52,380 --> 00:07:54,300
Pause the video if you need more time

178
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:55,180
to copy them.
